{#- Join the home WiFi as a fallback uplink for when this node's ethernet link loses carrier.
    netplan's YAML parser reads JSON, and a `format: json` output is the only form in which
    homelab-secrets escapes each value for its string, so a quote or backslash in the password
    cannot change the file's meaning. JSON holds no comment, so the routing argument is here.

    The wired link stays preferred: its pinned routes sit at metric 100, and these at 600. The
    fallback wins only because the address pin sets ignore_routes_with_linkdown on the wired
    link, so a route lookup skips its pinned routes once the cable drops (#4510). networkd gives
    the link's IPv6 RA route the same metric, through `[DHCP] RouteMetric` (#4561).

    server_ip stays on the wired link with no carrier (`ignore-carrier`), and the kernel answers
    ARP for it on any link (arp_ignore 0), so a peer that re-resolves server_ip with the cable out
    reaches this node over WiFi at its usual address.

    `optional` keeps boot from waiting for association: systemd-networkd-wait-online would
    otherwise hold every unit ordered after network-online.target, k3s among them. The three
    `use-dns: false` keys keep the lease's resolvers out of the node's DNS, because a pod's
    resolv.conf holds at most 3.

    The installed name, 80-homelab-wifi-fallback.yaml, sorts after the installer's 00- and 50-
    files, so these keys merge on top of any `wifis:` entry the installer wrote for this link.
    test_netplan_wifi_fallback.py checks each rule on both nodes' rendered files. -#}
{
  "network": {
    "version": 2,
    "wifis": {
      "{{ k3s_node_wifi_link }}": {
        "dhcp4": true,
        "optional": true,
        "dhcp4-overrides": {"route-metric": 600, "use-dns": false},
        "dhcp6-overrides": {"use-dns": false},
        "ra-overrides": {"use-dns": false},
        "access-points": {
          "{{ wifi_ssid }}": {"password": "{{ wifi_password }}"}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
